      up: {"title":"Serif, sans serif or display? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>This time most typefaces are described only by their features. Classify each by its structure, not by how it is being used.</p>","items":[{"t":"Vertical stress, extreme thick-thin contrast and hairline serifs with no brackets.","bin":0,"fb":"A Modern (Didone) serif."},{"t":"Strokes of almost even weight, a perfectly circular o, and no finishing strokes.","bin":1,"fb":"A geometric sans serif."},{"t":"Heavy, rectangular serifs and nearly even stroke weight, first designed for 19th-century posters.","bin":0,"fb":"A slab serif is still a serif."},{"t":"Letters that join together like formal copperplate handwriting.","bin":2,"fb":"A formal script: a display face for short use only."},{"t":"Open, pen-influenced shapes with slight variation in stroke width and no serifs.","bin":1,"fb":"A humanist sans serif."},{"t":"Diagonal stress, low contrast and bracketed, angled serifs from the broad-nib pen.","bin":0,"fb":"An Old Style serif."},{"t":"Letters shaped like palm fronds and steel pans for a festival headline.","bin":2,"fb":"A decorative display face."},{"t":"Franklin Gothic, a slightly irregular early sans serif, set very large as a headline.","bin":1,"fb":"It is a grotesque sans serif. Using a face for a headline does not make it a display face."},{"t":"Neutral, uniform strokes and horizontal stroke endings, common on airport signs.","bin":1,"fb":"A neo-grotesque sans serif, such as Helvetica."}],"win":"Well classified. Stress, contrast, serif shape and construction identify a typeface; how it is being used does not change its class."}
