      up: {"title":"Spot the kerning errors (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>A second Island Wave Travel proof has nine notes. Every note is a real spacing problem, but only some are fixed by kerning.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY note where kerning (adjusting one specific pair of letters) is the correct fix.","lines":["1. In the headline AVOCADO, the A and V look further apart than the other letters.","2. The all-caps label BOOK NOW feels cramped, with every letter equally tight.","3. In \"Yacht\", a hole opens because the a cannot tuck under the arm of the Y.","4. Justified text in a narrow column shows white channels running down the paragraph.","5. Lines of 110 characters make readers lose their place.","6. In the logo LT Water Taxi, the gap between L and T makes it read \"L T\".","7. Body text set 9 pt on 9 pt looks dark and cramped.","8. In \"Fwd\", the arm of the F leaves a gap before the w.","9. One word sits alone as the last line of a paragraph at the top of a column."],"correct":[0,2,5,7],"win":"Correct. Notes 1, 3, 6 and 8 are single-pair gaps (AV, Ya, LT, Fw): kerning. Note 2 needs more tracking (all caps need opening up), note 4 is rivers (change justification or hyphenation), note 5 is the measure, note 7 is leading, and note 9 is a widow.","miss":"Kerning fixes the space between ONE pair of letters. Spacing across a whole word is tracking; space between lines is leading; line length is the measure; white channels are rivers; a stranded line is a widow."}
