     up: {"title":"RGB, CMYK or RYB? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>These statements describe how each model works, not just where it is used. Sort each by the colour model it describes.</p>","items":[{"t":"Starts from black and adds light to make colours brighter.","bin":0,"fb":"Additive mixing starts from no light: RGB."},{"t":"Starts from white paper; each layer of ink removes more of the reflected light.","bin":1,"fb":"Subtractive mixing with inks: CMYK."},{"t":"The model with the smaller gamut, so some vivid screen colours cannot be reproduced in it.","bin":1,"fb":"Four process inks reproduce fewer colours than an RGB screen."},{"t":"Each channel is stored as a number from 0 to 255.","bin":0,"fb":"Red, green and blue channels each run from 0 to 255 (00 to FF in hex)."},{"t":"Blue and yellow paint are mixed to make green sea-grape leaves for a mural.","bin":2,"fb":"Mixing pigments on the traditional wheel: RYB."},{"t":"Values such as C 100, M 0, Y 30, K 0 written on the printer's job sheet.","bin":1,"fb":"Percentages of four inks describe a CMYK colour."},{"t":"The historical artist's wheel used in art classes to show complementary pairs such as red and green.","bin":2,"fb":"Red and green as complements belong to the traditional RYB wheel."},{"t":"Cyan appears where green and blue light overlap on a projector screen.","bin":0,"fb":"Overlapping green and blue light makes cyan: additive RGB."},{"t":"A separate black plate is added for sharp text and deep shadows.","bin":1,"fb":"K (key, black) is the fourth plate in CMYK printing."}],"win":"Well sorted. RGB adds light from black; CMYK subtracts light from white paper and has a smaller gamut; RYB is the traditional pigment wheel."}
